ARTICLE 5 - ANTI-FRAGMENTATION
5.1  A public authority shall not divide a materially unified decision into
successive actions for the purpose or effect of avoiding a statutory
threshold.
5.2  For two registered actions a and b, the IRA shall calculate a
Subject-Overlap Index:
   F(a,b) =
      w_O O(a,b)
    + w_P P(a,b)
    + w_D D(a,b)
    + w_T T(a,b)

 subject to:

   w_O + w_P + w_D + w_T = 1
   w_i >= 0

 where:

   O(a,b) = overlap of objective-function families
   P(a,b) = overlap of affected populations
   D(a,b) = overlap of material data inputs
   T(a,b) = overlap of decision type.

5.3  Each component shall be normalized to:
   O,P,D,T in [0,1].

5.4  The IRA shall publish the operational methodology used to calculate
each component, including weighting rules, normalization procedures,
uncertainty treatment, and reproducibility requirements.
5.5  A fragmentation alert shall be issued where:
   F(a,b) > 0.60

 AND

   a and b concern substantially related public decisions
   registered within a rolling 180-day period.

5.6  The 0.60 threshold is a statutory trigger, not an estimate of
mathematical truth. The IRA may propose recalibration only through the
procedure established in Article 53.
5.7  An alert is not itself a finding of unlawful fragmentation. The affected
authority shall receive notice and an opportunity to provide a reasoned
response.
5.8  Where the IRA determines that fragmentation was intentional or had the
effect of evading a statutory requirement, the affected actions shall be
treated as one decision for threshold purposes.
5.9  Re-characterization, sequencing, or splitting shall not defeat the
substantive application of this Title.
