ARTICLE 8 - CITIZEN PRIORITY-SETTING
8.1  Before formal problem formulation, a Full-Cycle matter shall undergo
citizen priority-setting.
8.2  The purpose is to identify:
   (a) concerns;
   (b) harms;
   (c) affected interests;
   (d) values;
   (e) questions that citizens consider deserving of consideration.

8.3  Priority-setting shall NOT:
   (a) constitute the legally operative problem statement;
   (b) define the administrative decision boundary;
   (c) prescribe the final objective function;
   (d) authorize consequential AI execution.

8.4  The output shall be a Priority Brief.
8.5  The Priority Brief is an input into formal human framing, not a
substitute for it.
8.6  Where consensus is absent, a dissenting minority statement shall be
preserved and published.
8.7  Step 3A therefore answers:
   "Which concerns deserve consideration?"

 while Step 0 answers:

   "What legally operative problem is the public authority formally
    constituted to address?"

8.8  No legal consequence shall attach to the Priority Brief as though it
were itself a formal problem statement.
