ARTICLE 9 - ARTICLE 12 FRAMING AUDIT
9.1  The framing audit shall contain:
   (a) Primary Problem Statement;

   (b) at least three distinct Rejected Alternative Framings;

   (c) at least one rejected framing originating from a Dissenting
       Expert Lot;

   (d) Distributive Impact Analysis;

   (e) Normative Assumptions;

   (f) Objective-Function Provenance;

   (g) Priority Brief Integration;

   (h) reasoned explanation for any departure from the Priority Brief.

9.2  The Distributive Impact Analysis shall address, as applicable:
   (a) socio-economic effects;
   (b) environmental effects;
   (c) rights-related effects;
   (d) affected populations;
   (e) trade-offs;
   (f) reallocations.

9.3  The audit shall identify assumptions concerning:
   (a) efficiency;
   (b) growth;
   (c) equity;
   (d) sustainability;
   (e) other materially normative criteria.

9.4  Objective-function provenance shall identify whose values, priorities,
constraints, and trade-offs are encoded in the optimization target.
9.5  No objective function shall be treated as normatively neutral merely
because it is expressed mathematically.
