ARTICLE 16 - MACHINE LEGITIMATION AND HUMAN REASONING
16.1 A machine assertion shall not constitute an independent source of legal
legitimacy.
16.2 Statements such as:
   "society desires X"
   "the data establishes Y"

 shall have no legal force merely because they were generated by an
 automated system.

16.3 No consequential decision may rest solely upon a machine-asserted
normative or empirical premise.
16.4 Human-authored reasoning shall identify:
   (a) the material premises;
   (b) the evidence relied upon;
   (c) relevant uncertainty;
   (d) normative choices;
   (e) reasons for accepting or rejecting material AI-generated claims.

16.5 Empirical evidence, predictive models, and AI-generated analyses remain
admissible inputs to human deliberation.
