ARTICLE 17 - NULLITY, MATERIALITY, RELIANCE AND REDRESS
17.1 A consequential act taken without a required prior compliant and
published audit is void ab initio, subject to the Reliance Rule.
17.2 The following constitute strict defects:
   (a) omission of an entire mandatory deliverable;
   (b) omission of one of the required rejected framings without a
       reasoned record;
   (c) consequential execution without the required certificate or
       valid emergency authority.

17.3 Minor formal defects that do not materially impair contestability may
be cured with notice.
17.4 Standing includes:
   (a) natural persons;
   (b) legal persons;
   (c) civil-society organizations;
   (d) affected individuals;
   (e) parliamentary minorities representing at least 10 percent of
       the relevant chamber.

17.5 A court may issue an interlocutory injunction halting:
   (a) automated processing;
   (b) option generation;
   (c) administrative implementation.

17.6 Where immediate cessation would itself endanger life or critical
services, the court shall order a defined safe state or degraded mode
rather than an undefined freeze.
17.7 GOOD-FAITH RELIANCE
 Third-party acts performed in good faith shall not automatically become
 void.

 A court may:

   (a) preserve temporal effects;
   (b) preserve an act where nullification would be disproportionate;
   (c) order compensation against the responsible agency.

 Benefits already lawfully paid shall not be clawed back from recipients
 acting in good faith solely because of an upstream procedural defect.

17.8 INDIVIDUAL REDRESS
 The agency shall notify affected persons of:

   (a) the decision;
   (b) applicable redress rights;
   (c) the date from which the limitation period runs.

 The claim period is ninety days from notice.

 Available remedies include:

   (a) re-examination by an uninvolved human decision-maker;
   (b) compensation for material harm caused by a defective audit;
   (c) compensation where an emergency is subsequently determined to
       have been unjustified.

17.9 Redress is independent of nullity and survives procedural cure.
