ARTICLE 21 - DECLARATION AUTHORITY AND SEPARATION OF POWERS
21.1 An emergency may be declared by:
   (a) the head of executive authority; OR
   (b) an authorized director-level agency official.

21.2 The declaration shall not itself confer unlimited operational authority.
21.3 The declaration authority and operational authority shall be separately
identified where practicable.
21.4 An emergency declaration does not automatically authorize:
   (a) expansion beyond the declared hazard;
   (b) unrelated data collection;
   (c) modification of the objective function;
   (d) expansion to new populations;
   (e) unrelated model deployment;
   (f) use of outputs as permanent policy precedent.

21.5 Each such action requires independent legal authority.
21.6 The declaration shall contain:
   (a) specific non-speculative factual grounds;
   (b) named Agency Responsible Officer;
   (c) ex-ante named Deputy;
   (d) applicable hazard template or ad hoc indicator set;
   (e) scope of authorized emergency use.

