ARTICLE 23 - EMERGENCY SUNSET
23.1 The initial Emergency Path expires at:
   23:59:59 UTC on the thirtieth calendar day.

23.2 One renewal of no more than thirty additional calendar days is permitted
only upon:
   (a) supermajority approval of the designated oversight body; OR
   (b) approval by the statutory mini-public.

23.3 The total Emergency Path duration shall never exceed sixty calendar
days.
23.4 The sixty-day ceiling includes all mandatory wind-down time.
23.5 No renewal may revive or extend an emergency after an event-triggered
sunset has lawfully commenced.
23.6 The following event triggers initiate sunset:
   (a) registered baseline metrics return to normal for seventy-two
       consecutive hours as independently measured by the IRA;

   (b) the Agency Responsible Officer becomes unreachable for six hours;

   (c) the Deputy becomes unreachable for six hours after assuming
       authority;

   (d) an independent expert lot finds that the declaration rests on
       stale or materially non-representative data.

23.7 If the Agency Responsible Officer is unreachable for six hours, the
named Deputy automatically assumes temporary authority.
23.8 If both are unreachable for six hours, the Emergency Continuity Officer,
designated ex ante by Parliament and independent of the agency and IRA,
assumes temporary control.
23.9 The IRA shall never operate the regulated system.
================================================================================
ARTICLE 24 - EMERGENCY WIND-DOWN
24.1 An event-triggered sunset does not terminate active processing
instantaneously.
24.2 A mandatory wind-down period of seventy-two hours shall begin.
24.3 The wind-down period is INCLUDED within the sixty-day cumulative
emergency ceiling.
24.4 During wind-down:
   (a) the system shall transition to safe state or degraded mode;
   (b) no new emergency decisions may be initiated;
   (c) the ex-post audit begins immediately;
   (d) existing protective functions may continue only to the extent
       required to prevent greater harm.

24.5 Wind-down shall not be interpreted as a new emergency authorization.
================================================================================
ARTICLE 25 - ANTI-CHAINING
25.1 No new Emergency Path may be declared concerning substantially similar
subject matter within 180 calendar days after expiry of the prior
Emergency Path.
25.2 The 180-day period runs from the final expiry of the prior emergency,
including its lawful wind-down.
25.3 Splitting, re-characterizing, or sequencing decisions to evade the
sixty-day ceiling or 180-day chaining prohibition constitutes
circumvention.
25.4 Circumvention results in nullity and may result in personal liability
under Article 18.
================================================================================
ARTICLE 26 - EMERGENCY SAFE HARBOR
26.1 Substantive good faith may suspend immediate nullity only where:
   (a) lodging was timely;
   (b) later evidence confirms that the emergency was non-speculative;
   (c) the declarant acted on the best information reasonably available
       at the time.

26.2 Late lodging is never covered by this safe harbor.
26.3 The safe harbor suspends nullity pending ex-post audit.
26.4 If the audit finds that the emergency was manufactured, materially
exaggerated, or knowingly unsupported, the safe harbor dissolves
retroactively, subject to the Reliance Rule.
26.5 Good-faith error is not equivalent to manufactured emergency.

26.6 PLAUSIBILITY PRE-COMMITMENT FOR AD HOC INDICATORS
   Where an emergency relies on an ad hoc indicator set under
   Article 20.3, the IRA shall issue a provisional plausibility
   assessment within twelve hours of lodging.
   If the IRA determines that the ad hoc indicators are structurally
   incapable of measuring the claimed hazard, the safe harbor under
   this Article is immediately voided, and the declaration is subject
   to absolute nullity.
================================================================================
ARTICLE 27 - LAW OF STOP
27.1 The Law of Stop establishes layered institutional authority.
27.2 L1 - DEVELOPER STOP
 Authority:
   Provider Responsible Officer, distinct from the Agency Responsible
   Officer.

 Trigger:
   internal red-team finding of catastrophic risk or material
   misalignment.

 Technical requirement:
   documented hard-lock capability within fifteen minutes.

27.3 L2 - REGULATOR STOP
 Authority:
   Independent Registry Authority.

 Trigger:
   breach of pre-registered failure thresholds or statutory conditions.

 Technical requirement:
   enforceable access to model weights and inference logs in a controlled
   audit environment.

27.4 L3 - INFRASTRUCTURE STOP
 Authority:
   designated critical-infrastructure authority.

 Trigger:
   failure of L1 and L2 or systemic cascade risk.

 Technical requirement:
   physical air-gap capability at the data-centre level.

27.5 L4 - JUDICIAL STOP
 Authority:
   competent court.

 Trigger:
   any statutory emergency or stop condition requiring judicial
   intervention, including urgency.

 Technical requirement:
   independent technical audit where ordered by the court.

---
ARTICLE 28 - SAFE STATE AND DEGRADED MODE
28.1 Every stop shall transition the system into a defined safe state or,
where justified, degraded mode.
28.2 Safe state requires:
   (a) no further consequential actions;
   (b) preservation of relevant state;
   (c) preservation of forensic evidence;
   (d) prohibition on destruction or alteration of model weights or
       relevant logs.

28.3 Degraded mode may be used only where complete cessation would create a
greater foreseeable harm.
28.4 Degraded mode permits only:
   (a) protective actions;
   (b) containment;
   (c) continuity of essential functions;
   (d) non-expansive operations.

28.5 The choice between safe state and degraded mode shall be justified
in writing and published on the Registry within six hours.

28.6 TECHNICAL BOUNDING OF DEGRADED MODE
   Degraded mode operations must be mathematically constrained to a
   pre-verified safe envelope. The system's control policy shall
   operate under a strictly bounded sub-optimality constraint
   (e.g., Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman safe envelope), ensuring zero
   degrees of freedom for optimization outside the defined
   life-preserving or containment action set.
================================================================================
ARTICLE 29 - RESTART AUTHORITY
29.1 Restart requires:
   (a) written root-cause report;
   (b) approval by the authority that ordered the stop;
   (c) twenty-four-hour cooling-off period after publication of the
       root-cause report.

29.2 Where the ordering authority is legally unavailable, the IRA may
authorize restart only where the original authority's legal function
has not been transferred elsewhere by law.
29.3 A final judicial order under L4 prevails over conflicting administrative
positions, subject to any safe-state or degraded-mode conditions imposed
by the court.
29.4 Restart without the required authorization constitutes unauthorized
processing.
================================================================================
ARTICLE 30 - DISAGREEMENT BETWEEN STOP LAYERS
30.1 Where two or more administrative stop layers disagree, the more
restrictive position prevails temporarily, subject to the safe-state
rule.
30.2 The IRA shall resolve the administrative disagreement within twenty-four
hours.
30.3 No administrative layer may unilaterally override another administrative
layer.
30.4 A final judicial order under L4 has controlling legal effect.
================================================================================
ARTICLE 31 - REACHABILITY AND DRILLS
31.1 Minimum reachability targets:
   safety-critical systems : 15 minutes
   other high-risk        : 2 hours
   general-purpose        : 24 hours

31.2 Unannounced drills on live safety-critical systems are prohibited.
31.3 Drills shall be conducted in shadow or staging environments that mirror
production sufficiently to test the relevant control pathway.
31.4 Semi-annual unannounced shadow-mode drills are mandatory.
31.5 Drill results shall be independently verified.
31.6 Drills shall include simulated L1-L4 failure scenarios.
31.7 A provider drill failure triggers:
   (a) review of provider certification;
   (b) corrective action;
   (c) an automatic Article 12 Full-Cycle audit for the deploying agency
       where the failure creates a material governance defect.

31.8 Revocation of provider certification shall include a controlled
transition period where immediate withdrawal would itself create
material operational risk.
================================================================================
ARTICLE 32 - EMERGENCY EX-POST AUDIT AND PRECEDENT
32.1 An emergency ex-post audit shall commence immediately after wind-down
and shall be completed within fifteen days where technically feasible.
32.2 The audit shall use lot-drawn accredited experts.
32.3 The report shall be provided to Parliament and the Data Protection
Authority and published subject to lawful confidentiality protections.
32.4 Public review shall address:
   (a) whether the emergency conditions were satisfied;
   (b) whether registered indicators performed as expected;
   (c) whether the intervention remained within scope;
   (d) whether the emergency should affect future metric design.

32.5 Emergency outputs shall not constitute precedent for future normal-path
decisions without a new Article 12 framing audit.
32.6 Historical reuse of an emergency output does not transfer the legal
legitimacy of the original emergency to a later decision.
================================================================================
ARTICLE 33 - EMERGENCY OUTPUT PROVENANCE AND TRAINING BAR
33.1 Providers shall attach machine-readable provenance markers to emergency
outputs.
33.2 Providers shall maintain a tamper-evident training-data manifest,
including hashed lists or equivalent integrity-preserving records.
33.3 Such records may be retained under controlled access or escrow where
public release would disclose protected information.
33.4 Downstream training pipelines shall preserve emergency provenance.
33.5 No model may be trained on emergency-path output for a specific
consequential use unless a fresh Article 12 framing audit has been
completed for that use.
33.6 Training on emergency output without the required fresh audit constitutes
unauthorized processing.
33.7 If downstream discovery establishes that a model was trained on
untagged or unauthorized emergency output, the responsible operator
shall:
   (a) suspend consequential use;
   (b) correct provenance records;
   (c) re-register the affected model;
   (d) obtain a fresh Article 12 audit.

33.8 Emergency output therefore does not become institutionally normalized
merely through data ingestion or model retraining.
================================================================================
ARTICLE 34 - TRADE-SECRET PROTECTION
34.1 Objective-function provenance may be redacted for a genuine trade secret
only where:
   (a) the redaction does not prevent assessment of distributive impact;
   (b) an unredacted version is supplied to the IRA under seal;
   (c) the redaction is time-limited;
   (d) the redaction is reviewed every twelve months.

34.2 The IRA shall engage at least one independent technical expert from the
Accredited Expert Pool for every redaction assessment.
34.3 The expert shall comply with the same conflict-of-interest requirements
applicable to IRA board members.
34.4 Blanket trade-secret claims are void.
34.5 A trade-secret claim shall identify the specific protected information
and shall not merely characterize an entire model or system as
confidential.
================================================================================
ARTICLE 35 - CONSEQUENCES OF MIS-TAGGING
35.1 Untagged or materially mis-tagged emergency output is void for
regulatory reuse.
35.2 The consuming model is not automatically void solely because it consumed
mis-tagged output.
35.3 The consuming model shall nevertheless:
   (a) suspend consequential use where required by Article 33;
   (b) correct provenance;
   (c) re-register;
   (d) undergo any required fresh Article 12 audit.

35.4 Continued operation after expiry of authorization constitutes
unauthorized processing.
================================================================================
ARTICLE 36 - INDEPENDENT REGISTRY AUTHORITY
36.1 The Independent Registry Authority is an independent public authority.
36.2 It shall not operate, develop, deploy, or commercially control regulated
AI systems.
36.3 Governance Board:
   9 members total:

   [3] constitutional/administrative law jurists
        nominated through the Judicial Council;

   [3] technical experts in systems and AI audit
        nominated through the National Academy;

   [3] citizen delegates
        selected by stratified lot.

36.4 Members serve non-renewable six-year staggered terms.
36.5 Three seats rotate every two years.
36.6 Integrity requirements include:
   (a) no prohibited commercial AI consultancy within five years before
       appointment;
   (b) no prohibited equity interest above the statutory threshold;
   (c) no incompatible executive office.

36.7 Post-service revolving-door restrictions apply for ten years.
================================================================================
ARTICLE 37 - IRA MANDATE AND JURISDICTIONAL LIMITS
37.1 Core functions:
   (a) Registry administration;
   (b) compliance verification;
   (c) Article 12 audit oversight;
   (d) Article 14 oversight;
   (e) L2 stop enforcement;
   (f) mini-public administration;
   (g) metric evaluation;
   (h) procedural recalibration within the limits of this Title.

37.2 The IRA may not:
   (a) create a new fundamental-rights obligation without legislation;
   (b) expand the substantive jurisdiction of this Title without
       legislative authorization;
   (c) create new criminal offences;
   (d) alter constitutional rights;
   (e) determine substantive policy preferences merely through
       recalibration;
   (f) operate the systems it regulates.

37.3 IRA procedural rules shall remain reviewable by the courts.
================================================================================
ARTICLE 38 - IRA INVESTIGATORY POWERS
38.1 Subject to judicial safeguards established by law, the IRA may require:
   (a) testimony;
   (b) relevant raw datasets;
   (c) pre-training corpora;
   (d) alignment records;
   (e) inference telemetry;
   (f) controlled access to model weights.

38.2 Physical or digital entry shall require the applicable warrant or other
legal authorization.
38.3 Model-weight access shall occur in a controlled audit environment where
feasible.
38.4 Investigatory powers shall be proportionate to the regulatory purpose
and shall remain subject to judicial review.
