ARTICLE 43 - EVIDENTIARY STANDARD
43.1 A court may vacate an L2 stop where clear and convincing evidence
establishes:
   (a) manifest technical or factual error;
   (b) bad faith;
   (c) gross technical error;

 OR where:

   (d) continuation of the stop creates direct, non-speculative,
       irreparable harm to human life or critical infrastructure that
       exceeds the foreseeable risk of automated decision failure.

43.2 A presumption of technical correctness applies only to IRA technical
findings that:
   (a) identify their evidentiary record;
   (b) disclose their methodology to the court;
   (c) are reproducible or independently auditable to the extent
       technically feasible.

43.3 The presumption does NOT apply to:
   (a) constitutional questions;
   (b) statutory interpretation;
   (c) jurisdiction;
   (d) procedural compliance;
   (e) substantive policy preferences.

43.4 Agency convenience shall not constitute sufficient grounds to dissolve
an L2 stop.
================================================================================
ARTICLE 44 - JUDICIAL DISPOSITIONS
44.1 AFFIRMANCE
 The L2 stop remains in force.

 The system enters:
   (a) Article 12 re-audit; OR
   (b) decommissioning where cure is not feasible.

44.2 CONDITIONAL CURE RELEASE
 Where direct irreparable harm under Article 43.1(d) is established,
 the court may authorize degraded operation subject to:

   (a) 100 percent human override;
   (b) daily IRA telemetry audit;
   (c) defined expiry;
   (d) restricted functionality.

44.3 VACATUR
 Where the statutory basis for the stop is disproven under Article 43,
 the court may dissolve the L2 stop.

44.4 Good-faith appeals shall not incur sanctions solely because they fail.
